We have derived improved differentiability properties for solutions to a non-autonomous obstacle problem, formulated as
  \begin{equation*}
  \min \left\{\int_{\Omega} |\nabla u|^{2} + \int_{\Omega} F(x,u): \text{u is twice differentiable in }\Omega, \\
  \nabla u \in L^{2}(\Omega,\mathbb{R}^{n}), \text{ and } u<\phi_{x}\text{ a.e. in }\Omega\right\}.
  \end{equation*}
  Under the sole assumption that the growth function $F$ satisfies the condition $|F(x,u)| \leq a(x)(1+|u|^{2}))$, where $a$ belongs to a specific Muckenhoupt class, we demonstrate that smooth solutions to the obstacle problem inherit certain regularity characteristics from the reference map $\phi$, namely that they possess the same order of differentiability as $\phi$ within the interior of the domain and enjoy a boundary regularity result analogous to $\phi$ up to a width of one dimension in the boundary.
